  "markdown": "## Business Model\nCostco operates membership warehouses and e-commerce sites based on the concept of offering low prices on a limited selection of nationally-branded and private-label products [10-K line 00134]. The company operates in multiple countries, including the U.S., Canada, Mexico, Japan, the U.K., Korea, Australia, Taiwan, China, Spain, France, Sweden, Iceland, and New Zealand [10-K line 00131]. Its warehouses are designed for efficiency with an average space of approximately 148,000 square feet, and they feature no-frills, self-service operations [10-K line 00139]. Costco purchases most merchandise directly from suppliers and routes it through cross-docking consolidation points or directly to warehouses [10-K line 00135].\n\n## Competitive Position\nCostco competes globally with a wide range of wholesalers and retailers, including supermarkets, supercenters, online retailers, gasoline stations, hard discounters, department stores, and specialty stores [10-K line 00190]. In the U.S., key competitors include Walmart, Target, Kroger, and Amazon [10-K line 00190]. Additionally, it competes with other warehouse clubs such as Sam’s Club and BJ’s Wholesale Club [10-K line 00190]. Costco differentiates itself through its membership model, which includes low prices, high-quality merchandise, and limited SKUs (less than 4,000 active SKUs per warehouse in core business) [10-K line 00141]. The company emphasizes private-label brands like Kirkland Signature, which contribute to lower costs and higher margins [10-K line 00192].\n\n## Growth Drivers\nCostco's growth is driven by increasing net sales through comparable sales growth, which is achieved through increased shopping frequency and average ticket size [MD&A line 00398]. Opening new warehouses contributes to growth, although this component becomes less significant as the warehouse base grows [MD&A line 00405]. Membership fee revenue, particularly from Executive memberships, also plays a role in profitability [MD&A line 00418]. The company leverages its large scale and volume purchasing power to maintain low gross margins while achieving high inventory turnover [10-K line 00134].\n\n## Catalysts\nKey catalysts for Costco include continued expansion of membership bases, particularly in international markets where growth is higher than in the U.S. and Canada [MD&A line 00415]. Increases in membership fees and upgrades to Executive memberships drive revenue growth [MD&A line 00459]. The company's e-commerce operations and digitally-enabled sales are growing significantly, representing 11% of total net sales in 2026 [10-K line 00153]. Additionally, the potential recovery of IEEPA tariff refunds positively impacts margins [MD&A line 00426].\n\n## Recent Developments\nIn fiscal year 2026, Costco opened 28 new warehouses (including three relocations), representing a net increase of 25 warehouses [MD&A line 00415]. Net sales grew by 10% to $297.2 billion, driven by comparable sales growth and sales at new warehouses [MD&A line 00416]. Membership fee revenue increased by 11% to $5.9 billion [MD&A line 00418]. The company also saw a 14% increase in net income to $9.226 billion, with diluted earnings per share reaching $20.76 [MD&A line 00422]. In April 2026, the Board of Directors approved a 13% increase in the quarterly cash dividend [MD&A line 00423].\n\n## Material Risks\nCostco faces several risks including economic conditions affecting consumer spending and inflation or deflation impacts on its operations [MD&A line 00398]. Currency fluctuations impact international operations, particularly in markets where sales are significant [MD&A line 00409]. Competition from global and national retailers is intense across various segments [10-K line 00190]. Rising costs related to employee compensation and benefits may affect profitability, although Costco aims to maintain competitive wages and benefits to retain employees [MD&A line 00407]. Tariffs on imported goods can impact merchandise costs [MD&A line 00403]. Furthermore, the company’s dependence on a low-margin business model makes it sensitive to changes in operational costs and pricing strategies [10-K line 00134].\n\n## Business Quality\nCostco demonstrates strong business quality through its focus on employee welfare, with competitive compensation and benefits, high retention rates (approximately 94% in U.S. and Canada for employees with over one year tenure), and a commitment to career development [10-K line 00181]. The company's operational efficiency, including low labor costs relative to sales volume, contributes to its ability to operate profitably despite low gross margins [10-K line 00139]. Its membership model reinforces loyalty and provides steady fee revenue, with high renewal rates (92.3% in U.S. and Canada) [10-K line 00167].\n\n## Competitive Advantage\nCostco’s competitive advantage stems from its membership model, which encourages customer loyalty and generates recurring revenue [10-K line 00157]. The company benefits from volume purchasing power, which allows it to offer low prices while maintaining operational efficiency [10-K line 00134]. Its private-label brand Kirkland Signature enhances differentiation and contributes to higher margins [10-K line 00192]. The strategic use of cross-docking distribution channels creates freight volume and handling efficiencies, lowering costs compared to traditional multi-step distribution methods [10-K line 00135].\n\n## Cyclical Exposure\nCostco's business is somewhat cyclical due to seasonal fluctuations, particularly increased net sales and earnings during the winter holiday season [10-K line 00132]. Economic conditions affecting consumer spending, including inflation or deflation, also influence its performance [MD&A line 00398]. Changes in gasoline prices impact both net sales and gross margins, with higher prices generally increasing sales but decreasing margin percentage [MD&A line 00404]. Currency fluctuations and global trade dynamics introduce additional cyclical risks [MD&A line 00409].",
